WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.460
GEIR ENGDAHL: My
name is Geir Engdahl.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.240
I am CTO and
co-founder of Cognite.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.400
With me I have Carter Page.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.970
And we're going to
talk about how Cognite

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.480
is using Google Cloud
technologies to enable machine

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.940
learning on industrial data.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.590
And in particular, we'll
talk about time series data

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.460
and Bigtable as two of the
key technologies and problems

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.730
that we are solving.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.590
So a little bit
about the Cognite.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.470
We're a young company,
less than two years old,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.960
just crossed 100 employees.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.320
We're working with our
asset-intensive industries.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.350
So that basically means
large industrial companies

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.840
that have big machinery
that costs lots of money--

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.870
a lot in the oil and gas
vertical, also in shipping.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.510
And our mission is to
liberate industrial data

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:08.800
from silos and piece
that data together

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.880
to form a model of
industrial reality

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.990
so that humans and machines
can make better decisions

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.140
and take better actions.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.300
So it's a model that's
real time and historic

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.960
to have both the present
state of what's going on.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.500
And it has all the
previous data too,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.000
which is important if you want
to try to predict the future.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.620
So what exactly is an
industrial reality model?

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.000
Well, I'll try to
show a little bit.

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:56.170
It depends on how you view it.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:00.130
So there are many angles to
kind of look at this model.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.670
This is a typical operator view.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.540
So if you're in the control
room in an industrial plant,

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.759
this is very close to what
you would typically see.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.690
This is data now streaming
in live from the North Sea.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.080
It has about 1 to
2 seconds delay.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:22.270
It is data that is concerning
a single tank outside on an oil

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.450
platform-- or inside actually.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.640
The tank is called 20VA0002.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.510
And the typical
oil platform will

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:38.080
contain anywhere from 10,000 to
100,000 sensors, time series,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.170
like this.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.420
So here you have a handful.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:46.410
But it's just a tiny
piece of a huge machinery.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:51.510
So this is the kind of real
time what's happening right now.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.300
You also want to
see, as an analyst,

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.790
what has happened in the past.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.280
Each one of these squiggly lines
represents about 1 gigabyte

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:00.780
of data.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.700
This is one year of data.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.210
I really like this chart.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.250
It's my new hobby
to play with it.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.295
It's kind of like Google Earth.

00:03:11.295 --> 00:03:16.340
I can zoom in and view the
data at any resolution.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.830
And you know, given that this
is about 10 gigabytes of data,

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.950
you probably noticed by now
that the next Wi-Fi doesn't

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.820
support downloading all
that data this fast.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.410
So you need a back end that
can quickly crunch the data

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.910
and give you the data at the
resolution that you want.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.410
And you can go all
the way down here

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.070
to the rural data
points, which will pop up

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.070
when you zoom in enough.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:49.500
And of course, you can view
this in different ways.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.080
So this is a view that
humans tend to like.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.330
It's a three-dimensional view.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:59.300
We imported the entire CAD
model of the oil platform.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.170
And we connected it
with all the other data.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.670
So for instance, if we want
to see the tank that we just

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:15.190
viewed data from, which is
so aptly named 20VA0002,

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:19.140
you can see exactly where that
is, and what it looks like,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.730
and what it's connected to.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:27.400
So you kind of browse the
data in the three dimensions.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.100
Just to give you a
little impression of what

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.820
we mean by industrial
reality model,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.380
I want this model to be
up-to-date and contain

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:43.130
the data today and for
what happened in the past.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:47.400
By the way, the charting
library that I just showed you,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.570
we couldn't find that.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.060
So we had to make it.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.190
And we open-sourced it.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.560
So if you're interested,
you can use that.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.170
It's not tightly coupled
to the Cognite back end.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:01.060
So if you have any
provider that can give you

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.270
data at different solutions
you can use that--

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.890
[? uses react ?] on D3.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.550
So the scale of data
to be ingested is huge.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:15.720
And it's growing very fast.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.280
If you look, Cognite handles
lots of different data types

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.930
to build the model of
industrial reality.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.790
So that can be ERP data.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.040
It can be maintenance logs.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.640
It can be 3D models,
like you saw.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.880
But if you look at
the data by volume,

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:39.390
99.7% of the data that we
have is time series data.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.370
So that's really where
the huge data is.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.320
Even though the 3D
model was large--

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.840
it was about 10 gigabytes--

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:50.020
the time series
data dwarfs that.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.870
And it's exploding.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.639
And all of that time series
data need to go somewhere.

00:05:54.639 --> 00:05:55.555
It needs to be stored.

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:57.610
It needs to be processed.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.385
It needs to be queryable.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.460
So how do we do that?

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:05.480
What is under the hood?

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:13.410
So when we started
out building Cognite,

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.540
we started with
a few principles.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:17.850
And one of them is impact.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:21.300
And it kind of seems
strange to say this.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:28.260
But you have to kind of write
it down to for it to matter.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:32.820
It's easy as a
technologist to build

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.910
technology for technology's
sake because its cool.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.540
And I've been guilty
of that in the past.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.270
We've been lucky to
have large customers,

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.690
demanding customers,
very early on to guide us

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:51.602
in finding out what the real use
cases are to create real value.

00:06:51.602 --> 00:06:52.560
And then there's speed.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.540
So you want to show
something as fast as possible

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.760
so you can iterate,
you can get feedback.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.010
And those two put together,
there's a consequence to that.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.740
And that is that you want to
use managed services wherever

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.160
you can, especially for
anything that is stateful.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.940
Because handling stateful
storage service that

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.340
has to scale up
and down, that has

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.640
to have backups,
have redundancy,

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.680
have all the logs for who
accesses the data, et cetera--

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.420
all of that stuff is just
painful to implement.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.920
And it's going to slow you down.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.950
And it's not something that--

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:34.380
yeah, it's being done.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:38.550
So we recognized very early
that we needed a time series

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.790
database.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.080
And our hypothesis was
that we could get this

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.240
as a managed service
too, that there

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.680
would be something out of the
box as an API support of this.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.210
And our requirements were that
it would be robust and durable.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:56.510
So it means that
we don't drop data.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.330
No data points
should be dropped.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:03.570
It would have to support a huge
volume of reads and writes--

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:04.680
writes in particular.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.320
You always get new data in.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.720
Low latency, so they
can show the real time

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:12.210
version of what's going on--

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:16.450
you want to see data
at any time scale.

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:19.110
So you want the zoomed out
view and the zoomed in view

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.570
that I showed you.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.880
You want to be able to
efficiently backfill.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.830
So if you're onboarding
a new customer,

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:28.680
and that customer has a million
data points per second being

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.480
generated, and you
can handle 2 millions,

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.549
then backfilling is going
to take a long time.

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:35.549
If they have a year of
data for you to backfill,

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:37.423
it's going to take
another year before you're

00:08:37.423 --> 00:08:39.780
done with that, because you're
going to spend 1 million

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.990
of your capacity on the new data
and then 1 million per second

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:45.150
on the old.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.830
And you want to be
able to efficiently map

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.750
over data in order--

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:56.850
so the sequential reads for
training models, for instance.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:01.740
So we experimented with the
OpenTSDB at the beginning.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.040
It's a great piece of software.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.050
The cool thing is you
can use a Bigtable, which

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:10.560
is a managed storage back end.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.380
So you can use OpenTSDB with
Bigtable as the back end.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:18.900
Which is very nice, but
it had a few shortcomings.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.790
For instance, it's not durable.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:27.554
That means if you send a piece
of data to the data point,

00:09:27.554 --> 00:09:29.970
it will acknowledge that it
got the data point before it's

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.655
written, which that means
you can potentially lose data

00:09:32.655 --> 00:09:34.030
if you're scaling
it up and down.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.710
And it essentially
used a front fill path

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:46.350
for batch backfills, which made
backfilling very inefficient.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:50.200
And there were a few
other things as well.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.710
So we chose to build
our own time series

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.260
logic on top of Bigtable.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.010
So Bigtable is a fully managed
service, which, as you know,

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.000
we really wanted.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.560
It supports a huge number of
reads and writes for node.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.200
It's been tried and tested
on very large user facing

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:13.800
distributed systems at Google.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:17.930
And it has this property which
most time series databases

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:19.410
don't--

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.790
most key value
stores don't have--

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:23.820
which is that you can
scan forward efficiently.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:27.540
The keys are stored in order.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.470
A lot of key value
stores will hash the keys

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:35.650
so that you get the
load distributed evenly.

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.470
But Bigtable doesn't do that.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.580
So that means you don't
have to jump around when

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:40.960
you're reading sequential data.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.290
It also means that--

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.020
the flip side of that-- is that
you can run into situations

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.040
where you get hotspotting.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.220
So you need to write
your code around that.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:53.820
But for us, it's a price that--

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.770
it's been worth it for us.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.830
So I'll hand it over
to Carter Page here,

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:03.150
who is senior engineering
manager for Cloud Bigtable.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:05.760
CARTER PAGE: Thanks, Geir.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.581
So I'll talk a
little bit about--

00:11:07.581 --> 00:11:10.407
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.390
I'll talk a little bit
about Cloud Bigtable--

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:16.540
how it's a good fit
for IoT and why we see

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.790
more customers coming to it.

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:21.570
I do want to say I'm
particularly excited to be

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:22.570
presenting with Cognite.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:26.230
I think the stuff that
they're doing is really neat.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:31.930
I think his point about
doing impactful things,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.380
doing a comprehensive story
of IoT is very exciting.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.870
The idea of not just
connecting those devices

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:40.510
and getting the data, but
once you've got literally

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:41.710
tens of thousands devices--

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:45.520
way more than a single human
could actually monitor--

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.030
thinking about how do you
extract data, react to that,

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:54.810
and manage very high
risk asset situations.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:58.240
And he's going to get into some
really cool stuff after this.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.640
But let me talk a little
bit Cloud Bigtable

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:05.140
and how that is a good fit
for these types of use cases.

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:07.210
A quick show of
hands, just to get

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:10.150
a sense of the audience, who
is familiar with distributed

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:13.960
databases like Cassandra,
HBase, things like that?

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:14.460
OK.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.001
All right, so this
is not going to be

00:12:16.001 --> 00:12:18.820
rocket science to most people.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:22.630
The main thing, particularly
for large IoT use cases, where

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:26.560
people are looking at collecting
massive amounts of metrics,

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.890
is being able to handle this
really large scale traffic.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.640
So a couple of years
ago, for example,

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.300
we did a load test
with a company

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:40.420
where we basically simulated
the entire US trade markets

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:42.610
all being processed
together, which

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.270
we processed 25 billion
records in about an hour.

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:49.870
And that's capable just due
to the scale of Cloud Bigtable

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:50.590
and how it works.

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.550
We were peaking at about
34 gigabytes per second

00:12:54.550 --> 00:13:00.210
and about 34 million operations
per second on Bigtable

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:01.850
on a single instance.

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:05.050
And the reason this works is
because Bigtable was built

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:08.680
for very high scalability.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:11.620
And you essential get linear
characteristics way out

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:12.640
on the curve.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.160
So Bigtable was initially
designed by Google

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:17.500
as a backing store
for our crawler.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:22.030
And so it was stored to keep
a copy of the World Wide Web.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:23.320
It's expanded internally.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:26.330
It's being used for a lot
of other products as well.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.610
And so we have put about
14 years of engineering

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:34.660
into keep finding new upper
limits and breaking those.

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:38.597
So every distributed
system, eventually you

00:13:38.597 --> 00:13:40.180
have this straight
line that goes out,

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.890
and eventually it flattens out.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:44.020
Everything eventually
hits a bottleneck.

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:46.870
Or you might hit
something where it just--

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:49.750
if you've got an HBase
cluster with 1,000 machines,

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:52.070
you're going to hit
probabilistic machine failures,

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:55.030
and there's an overhead for
your operations and things

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:56.810
like that.

00:13:56.810 --> 00:14:00.790
So we will eventually flatten
out, but pretty far out,

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:05.380
and it would take a lot of work
for you to get up to the scale

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:06.850
where you would notice.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.302
The reason this is important--
the linear scaling,

00:14:09.302 --> 00:14:11.260
from a business perspective--
is this gives you

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:13.220
predictable cost of revenue.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:15.880
So when you're thinking
about building a system

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:17.860
and you're like, I've
got a terabyte of data,

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:20.020
and now this has to go to
10 terabytes, a petabyte,

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.870
10 petabytes, usually if
you're building this on top

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:25.580
of your own home-managed
Cassandra system,

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:28.480
you are going to have to rethink
each time you hit one of these

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:29.170
new tiers--

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:30.550
all right, now, how am I
going to deal with this?

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.140
I've got a lot more machines.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.357
I'm going to need a
new on-call rotation.

00:14:34.357 --> 00:14:36.940
I'm going to need new strategies
to be able to deal with this.

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.832
Here it's just a matter
of cranking up your nodes.

00:14:39.832 --> 00:14:41.290
And the number of
nodes you need is

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:45.580
proportional to the
throughput that you need.

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:48.490
So give a quick overview for
how Cloud Bigtable works,

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:52.660
you have clients that basically
talk to a single front endpoint

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:54.490
that load bounces to the nodes.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:57.340
So you don't need to think
about address mapping or talking

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:01.220
to individual nodes themselves.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:03.760
I'll take that layer away and
talk a little bit about what's

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:05.260
going on underneath the covers.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:09.520
So the data itself is being
stored durably in an underlying

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:11.181
file system called Colossus.

00:15:11.181 --> 00:15:13.180
And the Bigtable servers
themselves are actually

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.630
not storing any data.

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:16.540
They are taking
the responsibility

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:17.710
for serving the data.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:21.160
And every row is assigned
to only one node.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.230
So your entire
keyspace is basically

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:25.370
balanced across these
different nodes.

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:27.550
And this allows
a single entity--

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.070
by being responsible
for an individual row--

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:34.070
allows for atomicity of
operations on it and allows for

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:34.945
read your own writes.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:42.220
The advantage here of having
the file system and the data

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.716
dissociated, is it allows
us to do some clever things

00:15:45.716 --> 00:15:47.590
in terms of being able
to rebalance workloads

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:49.060
very aggressively.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:52.510
So you may have a
customer that has changed

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.940
their underlying workload,
which then impacts

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:56.470
how you are using Bigtable.

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:58.024
Or you may have
diurnal patterns.

00:15:58.024 --> 00:15:59.440
You may have
different things that

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.410
are coming on and going off
during the middle of the day,

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.830
changes which tablet
servers or nodes are

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:08.080
getting more or less activity.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.000
And what we'll do is we'll
actually identify these changes

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:12.060
in patterns.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:17.910
And we will just reassign areas
of data to different nodes.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.440
And so this allows
a couple of things--

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:23.880
one is it allows you to
not have to worry too much

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:25.770
about per node hotspotting.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:29.490
You can hotspot individual
rows, which is a problem.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.220
But in terms of getting
unlucky and having

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.770
one server that's
hotter than the rest,

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:36.750
we'll balance that out.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.820
It also means
higher utilization.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:41.520
And by having higher
utilization in these nodes,

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.394
by keeping things
balanced, you're

00:16:43.394 --> 00:16:45.310
not having the provision
for the hottest node.

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:47.643
We're trying to keep all the
nodes fairly well balanced.

00:16:47.643 --> 00:16:51.210
And that actually
means cheaper service

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:54.828
relative to running this on
an HBase cluster or Cassandra

00:16:54.828 --> 00:16:56.190
cluster.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:17:00.030
In addition to rebalancing, you
can resize up and down fairly

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:00.990
trivially.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:04.369
We have some customers who might
have ingestion workloads which

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:05.477
only need a few nodes.

00:17:05.477 --> 00:17:07.560
And they might run a batch
at the end of the month

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:08.460
or the end the week.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:12.390
And they might want to scale
that up to say 300 nodes.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:16.140
And you can do that
fairly instantaneously.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:17.970
If you've got a
really large data set,

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:21.150
it may take 10 to 20
minutes for the data

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.220
to rebalance over
the nodes just added.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.000
But it could be a good way to
make those batch jobs you run

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.620
once a week run really fast.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.450
When you're done, you
scale it back down again.

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:38.070
The basic data
model, it's key value

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:40.560
but has more dimensions to it.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.440
So you have a single index,
which is your row key.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.940
And then your data
is stored in columns.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:49.080
And the columns are a
tuple of basically a column

00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:51.090
qualifier and a column family.

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:53.400
The column family is
defined in your schema.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:56.820
And the column qualifier is
defined at insertion time.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:58.050
The table is sparse.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:02.610
So any column family,
column qualifier tuples

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:05.100
that you don't fill
in for a given row

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:09.150
don't cost against
your actual space.

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:11.650
The database is also
three-dimensional.

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:15.000
So under each of those
cells is an arbitrary number

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:16.080
of versions.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:21.540
So you can keep them there
pretty much indefinitely,

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:24.930
as long as you're row doesn't
get beyond a couple of hundred

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:25.980
megs.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.100
Or you can instill a
garbage collection, say,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.650
when you wipe out any
data that's over a week,

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:34.020
or just keep the last
five versions or something

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:36.540
like that.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.370
Wednesday, yesterday,
we announced

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.290
that we have replication now.

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:44.070
So between two
Bigtables in a region,

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.170
we will replicate the
data between them.

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:48.520
This has a few advantages.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:50.929
The first is it expands
your failure domain.

00:18:50.929 --> 00:18:53.220
So you're no longer [INAUDIBLE]
into the failure domain

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:54.390
of a single zone.

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:56.550
You've got two zones
with the failure domains.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.740
So that gives you
higher availability.

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.020
And another advantage that
people use, particularly

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:02.890
because the replication
is asynchronous,

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:04.680
is workload isolation.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:08.520
So some customers may have their
critical low latency server

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:12.270
loads on one cluster and then
maybe doing batch reloads

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:13.350
on another.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.660
And by doing it
on the other one,

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:19.080
they're not interfering
with each other essentially.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:22.380
The effective result of this,
on the high-availability side,

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.465
is we get an extra
9 onto our SLA.

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:32.192
We have a high availability
application policy.

00:19:32.192 --> 00:19:33.900
I won't totally get
into those right now.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:35.040
But you can go read up on these.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:36.456
You have application
policies that

00:19:36.456 --> 00:19:38.580
can define how you want
your traffic to be routed.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:41.310
And if you use the
high availability one,

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.170
you'll get automatic
zero-touch failover.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.180
So if there's any problems
in the middle of the night,

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:46.346
you don't have to get paged.

00:19:46.346 --> 00:19:49.860
It'll just failover for you.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:54.390
Being a large data
tool and a database

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:56.900
that's designed for terabytes--

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:59.910
petabytes, actually-- we
need really powerful tools

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:03.600
to be able to make the most
advantage of your database.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:06.750
And so we've got deep
integrations with BigQuery

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:08.830
with Dataproc, Dataflow.

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:11.790
BigQuery, the queries
are not as fast

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:14.190
as if you're going it's native
BigQuery, because BigQuery

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:15.570
is an offline store.

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:17.530
And certain
optimizations of BigQuery

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:20.100
to be online to be able to
get single-digit milliseconds

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:22.290
latency, which makes
the BigQuery queries

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:23.520
run a little slower.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:27.660
But it's nice because you can
do ad hoc queries on your data

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:29.654
without having to
write a MapReduce job.

00:20:29.654 --> 00:20:31.320
If you do want to
write a MapReduce job,

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:33.030
you can use Cloud
Dataproc, or you

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:36.240
can use our internal
replacement for MapReduce, which

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.072
is housed inside of Dataflow.

00:20:38.072 --> 00:20:39.780
And then also this
week, we're announcing

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:44.220
that we have a deep first order
integration with TensorFlow,

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.030
which just got put on to GitHub.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:48.780
And so people can start
playing with that.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.604
So I'll hand it
back to Geir, here.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.160
GEIR ENGDAHL: Thank you.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:21:00.510
So with that background, I'll
go into a little bit of detail

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:05.910
on how Cognite is using Cloud
Bigtable to store its data.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:09.360
And then we'll move on
into machine learning.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:13.480
And eventually you'll see what
the [? windmill ?] can do.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:18.760
So Carter talked about the
data model of Bigtable.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:22.690
This is our basic data schema.

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:24.480
So the first thing
that is very important

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:29.310
is how you choose your row keys,
because that's the only thing

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:35.080
that you can look up fast.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:40.860
So our row keys consist of a
sensor ID plus a timestamp,

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:43.590
or a time bucket.

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:47.440
That means that you
can look up, very fast,

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:51.590
the values for a particular
sensor at that particular point

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:52.090
in time.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:58.430
And you can also then scan
through all the values

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:00.890
of the sensor in order.

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:03.300
Which is nice if you
want to train a model.

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:05.948
And that's a very
inexpensive operation.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:12.470
Inside of each row, we
store more than one value.

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:15.320
It's not just one value per row.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:21.380
We stuff a lot of data points
inside each row, typically

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:26.630
around 1,000 data
points per row.

00:22:26.630 --> 00:22:32.810
So you have the timestamps,
which are unique timestamps.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:35.700
And then you have values,
which are fielding points.

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:38.080
And of course it's binary stuff.

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:41.450
It's just drawn out in
readable numbers here.

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:43.520
But it's all binary
to save space.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:48.080
And actually, Bigtable does
compression for you too.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:51.830
So what you'll see
if you stop ingesting

00:22:51.830 --> 00:22:54.090
new data into your
Bigtable instance,

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:56.690
you'll see that the total
size of it goes down.

00:22:56.690 --> 00:22:59.550
Which can be scary at first if
you don't know what's going on.

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.160
Where's my data going?

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:02.540
But it's actually a good thing.

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:12.840
Here's how we architected
our data ingestion pipeline.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:18.470
So every step along this
path is auto scaling.

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:21.440
Carter talked about how easy
it is to scale Bigtable.

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:23.390
And we have a service
which looks at the load

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:25.670
and then scales it up and down.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:27.590
It's pretty simple logic.

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:32.520
So it starts with
Cloud load balancing.

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:35.730
And then an API node, which
is a Kubernetes service, which

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:38.840
handles authentication
and authorization.

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:42.492
Then it will put the data
point onto a Pub/Sub queue.

00:23:42.492 --> 00:23:43.950
And then it will
say to the client,

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.866
we got your data-- we're
not going to lose it.

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:52.160
So Pub/Sub is another
component that we use a lot.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:55.130
It's a very nice
component in the way

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:58.160
that it scales to
whatever you ask for.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.650
If you look at the
documentation for Pub/Sub,

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:03.620
it will say like the limit
on the number of published

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:07.850
operation unlimited, and
subscriber operations

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:08.540
unlimited.

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:14.270
So that's kind of
a bold statement.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:18.260
We haven't drawn
into the limit there.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:21.290
Once it's on the queue, it
gets picked up by a subscriber

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:25.270
to that queue, which
will package the data

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:27.520
and write it to Bigtable.

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:32.930
So that's where our time
series writing logic lives.

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:38.170
And once it's been written there
is a new job put on the queue

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:39.900
to do with aggregates.

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:41.570
So those are
roll-ups that we use

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:47.240
to be able to answer queries
about any arbitrary time

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:49.180
scale efficiently.

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:52.220
So that's what we need in
order to do the dynamic zooming

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:56.470
that you saw at the beginning.

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:59.810
So your KPIs are
throughput and latency

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.090
for this kind of pipeline.

00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:04.780
It's typically
what you'd look at.

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:07.850
So the data that
comes in is queryable

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:10.880
after 200 milliseconds,
in the 99th percentile,

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:15.290
and we regularly handle 1
million data points per second.

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:18.170
And I'm pretty sure it could
do much more than that too.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:23.710
And querying, much simpler--

00:25:23.710 --> 00:25:25.620
this is in
synchronous operation,

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:28.140
so it goes to the API node
and then straight to Bigtable.

00:25:32.586 --> 00:25:33.960
One of the
optimizations that you

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:36.240
want to do if you want
to transfer a lot of data

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.510
here is that typically
API developers

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:42.150
like to have JSON data.

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:46.230
And for most applications like
Dashboard that makes sense.

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:49.457
If you're writing a Spark
connector to your API,

00:25:49.457 --> 00:25:51.290
and you want to run
machine learning on that

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:55.110
and transfer lots of data,
then the JSON serialization--

00:25:55.110 --> 00:25:57.380
the serialization
becomes an issue.

00:25:57.380 --> 00:26:00.050
And so you want to use
something like Protocol Buffer

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:02.560
or another binary
protocol for that.

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:04.470
And it's not really
the size that matters.

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:07.200
Because if you GCF to JSON,
it's very small anyway

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:08.820
because it's very repetitive.

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:13.600
But it's the memory overhead
of doing that serialization.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:17.660
So that's a nice optimization.

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:22.170
I want to talk a little
bit about cleaning

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:24.420
of industrial data
because it's something

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.010
that's often overlooked.

00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:35.180
If you want to make
a useful application

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:37.490
in the industrial
IoT space, it's

00:26:37.490 --> 00:26:40.370
not enough to have
time series and AI.

00:26:40.370 --> 00:26:42.170
A lot of people are
running around saying

00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:47.090
time series plus AI is
going to solve everything.

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:51.082
A very simple question is if you
have 100,000 time series coming

00:26:51.082 --> 00:26:53.540
from an oil rig, and you want
to make your predictive model

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:58.760
for this one tank that
we saw, which time series

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:00.340
will you pick?

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:01.560
And how will you pick those?

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:03.750
Are you going to manually
go over all the diagrams?

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:06.660
It's going to take
you a lot of time.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:09.680
So typically, we see these
data science projects,

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:14.550
and they are really about
finding the right data.

00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:18.080
So that's what 80% of
the time is spent on.

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.590
And then at the
end of the project,

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:25.960
you have a wrapup where
you try to model something.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:29.270
If you want to truly
understand what's

00:27:29.270 --> 00:27:31.760
going on in the
industrial world,

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:35.150
you need to be able to get
data from a lot of sources--

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:38.990
data like the metadata of the
time series, the equipment

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:42.650
information-- who made
it, when was it replaced,

00:27:42.650 --> 00:27:48.500
failure logs from previously,
worked orders, 3D models,

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.000
and the process
information-- how

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.940
things are physically connected
and logically connected?

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:57.280
Which component is
upstream of this?

00:27:57.280 --> 00:28:00.030
And it's not enough to have
all this data in one place.

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.910
It needs to be connected.

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:03.410
The hard part is connecting it.

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:05.810
And the glue that
holds this together

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:10.010
is the object in
the physical world.

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:14.060
And the unfortunate thing is
that the same physical object

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:17.030
has a different name depending
on what system you ask.

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:24.710
So we spent a lot of time
on this contextualization,

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:27.440
figuring out how we'd
map the IDs from one

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:32.030
system to a unique
ID for each asset,

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:33.450
for each physical object.

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:38.510
So if you look at the
cleaning pipeline there,

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:41.480
there's this thing called
an asset matcher, which

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:43.670
we spent a lot of
time developing

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:48.340
and which will
assist experts and do

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:53.510
automatic mapping, in many
cases, of IDs from one system

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:57.420
to another to be able to make
this connected, contextualized

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:57.920
model.

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:09.800
So you're probably wondering
now what this windmill does,

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:12.050
and why it's here.

00:29:12.050 --> 00:29:14.170
So I'm just going to
say a little bit more,

00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:15.550
and then I'll get to it.

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:22.700
Predictive maintenance-- you've
probably all heard about this.

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:26.720
There is a great business case
for predictive maintenance.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:30.020
We have seen cases
where a single equipment

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:34.850
failure on a piece of subsea
equipment costs $100 million

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:37.160
to fix.

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:39.260
So obviously you
want to prevent that.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:46.730
But this is also why
it is so hard to do.

00:29:46.730 --> 00:29:49.540
Because the failures
are fairly rare.

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:51.500
There is not a
lot of label data.

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:56.810
Imagine what it would
cost to get enough label

00:29:56.810 --> 00:30:02.210
data to validate your
model, let alone train it.

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:07.010
So you're typically stuck with
these unsupervised approaches.

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:09.020
And for anomaly
detection, what we've seen

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:15.050
is two classes of approaches
for how to do this.

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:17.030
One is forecasting based.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:20.030
So it means that you will
take a set of sensors.

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:23.780
You will hide one of the values.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:27.560
And you'll try to predict
it using the others.

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:34.100
And if your prediction is far
from what is the actual value,

00:30:34.100 --> 00:30:38.440
then you flag that
as an anomaly.

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:43.240
Or the other approach is
you take your sensor data,

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:44.200
your set of sensors.

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:48.030
You plot them in an
n-dimensional space,

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:53.220
and you see what points
are close to each other.

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:56.870
They form clusters, and those
clusters typically represent

00:30:56.870 --> 00:30:59.170
different operational states.

00:30:59.170 --> 00:31:01.717
So you'll have a cluster
around the running stage.

00:31:01.717 --> 00:31:03.550
You'll have a cluster
around the idle stage.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:05.990
You'll have a cluster
around the powering,

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:09.240
powering down maintenance.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:11.410
And if you have a new
points, and it doesn't fit--

00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:14.560
it's far from any of
these cluster centers--

00:31:14.560 --> 00:31:16.753
then that it is an anomaly.

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:23.560
Let's look at this live.

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:34.180
So this demo is as
live as it gets.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:36.740
It has a lot of moving
parts literally.

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:42.870
Everything that you
see here is live.

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:45.490
There is no pre-trained model.

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:50.740
There is no pre-generated data.

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:56.080
The data is going to be
created right here, right now.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:32:01.070
And we'll train the model,
and we'll see if it works.

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:02.800
So are you excited?

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:03.640
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:04.348
GEIR ENGDAHL: OK.

00:32:04.348 --> 00:32:05.830
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:05.830 --> 00:32:06.330
Me too.

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:15.590
So let's see if we get data
from this wind turbine now.

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:16.950
And it looks like we do.

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:20.970
So this is a different
view of the wind turbine.

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:24.990
It's a 3D view into
what's going on there.

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:26.880
It has the sensor values.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.160
I can turn this knob.

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:31.570
I can increase the speed.

00:32:31.570 --> 00:32:36.300
You'll see it will start
to produce more energy.

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:39.360
It's producing a lot of energy
for a wind turbine the small.

00:32:42.650 --> 00:32:46.310
So let's go into Jupyter.

00:32:46.310 --> 00:32:48.920
I'm sure those of you
who are working with data

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:50.850
are kind of familiar with this.

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:55.490
So we're going to interact with
our Cognite API via our Python

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:58.490
SDK, which is also open source.

00:32:58.490 --> 00:33:01.000
So first we're going
to just log in here.

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:04.130
We're going to select
the right data.

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:07.580
And then we're going to plot it.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:14.350
This live plotter will show
the analyst's view of this.

00:33:14.350 --> 00:33:18.120
And you see if I
adjust the speeds,

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:20.400
you see it will go down.

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:27.100
If I take it up, it's
going to go up a bit.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:33.930
And this, what you
see on the screen,

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:35.892
is going to be
our training data.

00:33:35.892 --> 00:33:38.160
So I'm going to give it
a little bit more time.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:45.730
So it's seeing a little bit of
normal operation of this wind

00:33:45.730 --> 00:33:46.230
turbine.

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:52.000
We painfully brought this
wind turbine for you here.

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:53.040
It's 3D printed.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:55.800
It looks very homemade.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:58.920
We got it through
airport security.

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:04.350
I was taken aside by
security here at Google Next.

00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:07.380
They were wondering
what this base is.

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:10.590
Because this wind turbine
thing isn't mounted on it.

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:12.510
It has wires coming
out of it, and it

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:16.389
has this red scary light on it.

00:34:16.389 --> 00:34:20.969
So I've done a lot of
explaining to bring this here.

00:34:20.969 --> 00:34:25.310
Now I need to stop this
plotting to move on.

00:34:25.310 --> 00:34:29.230
I will create an anomaly
detector for this,

00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:33.340
and I will select a
time range for it.

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:37.949
Now this is pushing the training
operation up into the Cloud.

00:34:37.949 --> 00:34:41.179
So it doesn't actually
happen on my computer.

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:45.840
The SDK will just do an
API pull to train a model.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:48.480
And then we get the job
ID that we can query

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:51.053
for the status of the job.

00:34:51.053 --> 00:34:52.469
It is not a lot
of data right now,

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.860
so it took very
little time to train.

00:34:55.860 --> 00:35:02.750
And now we can create
another plotter.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:06.930
And it's going to plot again
live data from this wind

00:35:06.930 --> 00:35:08.520
turbine.

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:10.740
But this time, it's
also going to plot

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:18.080
the output of the predictor,
the anomaly detector.

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:21.090
So how can we introduce
an anomaly here?

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:24.860
Well, I'm going to
use brute force.

00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:27.110
I'm going to hold it back here.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:34.210
It takes a little bit
of time for the data

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:37.050
to appear in this Python thing.

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:39.430
And now you'll see it's
detected an anomaly.

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:41.196
You see the red
background there.

00:35:41.196 --> 00:35:43.992
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:51.640
And it should go back to normal
again once I've let it go.

00:35:51.640 --> 00:36:01.540
So there you have live
anomaly detection.

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:03.400
Now it's back to normal.

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:09.280
Operators can use
this to monitor--

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:12.160
if you want to monitor
a 100,000 time series

00:36:12.160 --> 00:36:13.575
you can't do that manually.

00:36:13.575 --> 00:36:14.950
You can't put it
up on the screen

00:36:14.950 --> 00:36:18.290
and have people watch it.

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:24.160
Well now, operators can be
alerted to strange conditions,

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:26.170
and they can look
into what's going on,

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:30.160
and hopefully prevent
the next $100 million

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:32.290
failure before it happens.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:42.780
So another very useful
thing that we do--

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:44.310
once you've detected
this anomaly,

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:46.340
your first question is
going to be something

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.930
like has this happened before?

00:36:48.930 --> 00:36:51.630
Or has something
similar happened?

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:56.450
So we implemented something
called similarity search

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:59.350
in the API, which is not
really machine learning,

00:36:59.350 --> 00:37:00.720
but it is a very useful thing.

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:04.230
And it is very computationally
expensive to do.

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:07.380
So you can take a time
period of a setup sensor

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:10.020
and look for that pattern in
a different set of sensors,

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:15.030
or in the same, find
similar portions.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:26.650
So this talk also has
"AutoML" in the title.

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:38.380
So AutoML-- on oil rigs
you have hydrocarbons.

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:43.150
And if there is a leak
and you have a spark,

00:37:43.150 --> 00:37:47.200
then it's potentially
very dangerous.

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:55.620
So they're always looking to
see if there are faulty wires.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:57.487
Faulty wires can
be very dangerous.

00:38:00.556 --> 00:38:01.930
So typically what
they do is they

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:05.320
have these regular inspections
every six months or every year,

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:06.640
where they go over everything.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:10.770
But these damaged wires,
they don't appear over time.

00:38:10.770 --> 00:38:14.090
They're typically the result of
someone stepping on something,

00:38:14.090 --> 00:38:20.670
or something mechanical happened
at a particular point in time.

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:22.170
So what we have
tried to do is build

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:26.410
a model which can
detect faulty wires so

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:30.250
that you can wear a
camera on the helmet

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:33.910
or use CCTV or other
ways of gathering images

00:38:33.910 --> 00:38:38.900
and in the background always
look for this kind of failure.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.860
I'm not trying to replace
those inspections.

00:38:41.860 --> 00:38:46.160
We are trying to augment
them and make it even better.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:50.860
So we trained the
model, using TensorFlow,

00:38:50.860 --> 00:38:57.880
to detect faulty wires and
also tell us where in the image

00:38:57.880 --> 00:38:59.110
the failure is.

00:38:59.110 --> 00:39:02.390
This is a project that
we spent three months on.

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:09.310
And we were able to get
to a particular accuracy.

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:13.690
That was about 95.5% on
precision and recall.

00:39:13.690 --> 00:39:17.890
And then in June, we
got access to AutoML.

00:39:17.890 --> 00:39:21.580
So we figured we would
upload the data set there

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:25.460
and see how well that would do.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:29.590
So I'll show what
that looks like.

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:39.140
So training a model
with AutoML, if you

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:41.930
can upload a bunch of
images onto a web page

00:39:41.930 --> 00:39:45.750
and you edit the CSB
file, they you can do it.

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:47.000
So we did that.

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:51.770
And in 5 minutes, I was able
to get a model with 96.1%

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:55.800
and 96.2% precision and recall.

00:39:55.800 --> 00:40:00.230
So that was half a
percentage point up.

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:03.750
But then we didn't give up.

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:05.600
We tweaked our model
a little bit more.

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:10.050
So we were able to get
that one even higher.

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:16.400
So we were able to get to
95% precision and 99% recall

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:20.540
on our own model, which
it's better on one

00:40:20.540 --> 00:40:21.950
metric but not the other.

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:27.170
But the big
difference, I think, is

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:30.830
that we spent three
months doing it,

00:40:30.830 --> 00:40:33.680
and it took five minutes
to train the AutoML model.

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:44.280
So let's see what that can do.

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:49.150
So on my way here, on
the first day at Moscone,

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:55.740
I found this wire outside,
and it looked dangerous.

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:04.320
So now I ran the AutoML on it.

00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:06.920
And it was not part
of the training set.

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:08.550
And this is kind of
a new hobby for me,

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:10.500
to go around and look
for faulty wires.

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:16.080
But yeah, it really
caught that quite well.

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:21.900
And on my way to the rehearsal,
which I did back in June,

00:41:21.900 --> 00:41:27.900
I also found this faulty
traffic light wire.

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:31.020
This is the knob that you push
to make the pedestrian light go

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:32.400
green.

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:36.580
And it also catches
that really well.

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:39.790
So I think it is
possible to do this.

00:41:39.790 --> 00:41:43.830
And I think there is a class
of problems that you can solve

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:46.490
in the industry--
like rust and leaks--

00:41:46.490 --> 00:41:52.480
and using camera
feeds as a sensor,

00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:57.300
that can be very powerful and
instrumental in getting people

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:58.080
out of harm's way.

00:42:08.900 --> 00:42:13.186
So I'll hand it over to
Carter to wrap it up.

00:42:13.186 --> 00:42:15.646
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:21.366
CARTER PAGE: Thanks, Geir.

00:42:21.366 --> 00:42:23.160
If we have any time--

00:42:23.160 --> 00:42:25.560
yeah, we'll have a little
time for questions after this.

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:29.490
I'm sure Geir will be
able to join us there.

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:33.840
So quick review again.

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:36.210
Cognite basically built
the system on top of GCP,

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:40.190
focusing on the scalability
and the throughput of Cloud

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:41.370
Bigtable.

00:42:41.370 --> 00:42:44.700
And focusing on the
capabilities of AutoML,

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:48.360
he was able to take a
training exercise which

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:50.430
took three months
with TensorFlow,

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:52.890
was able to do it in
five minutes with AutoML.

00:42:52.890 --> 00:42:55.050
Which is really
exciting for companies

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:57.990
that want to tap into
this type of technology

00:42:57.990 --> 00:43:01.470
without having to hire an
army of data scientists.

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:06.000
Not everyone can hire
Geir in their company.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:07.590
Also, one of the
things-- actually,

00:43:07.590 --> 00:43:09.881
I don't know if it was clear
when things were switching

00:43:09.881 --> 00:43:13.280
around when he
did the windmill--

00:43:13.280 --> 00:43:14.820
you saw some code.

00:43:14.820 --> 00:43:18.450
But he didn't actually program
the training model for that.

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:20.940
He just highlighted the
part that he had done before

00:43:20.940 --> 00:43:22.500
and said this is bad.

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:24.990
And so they were able to
put this out in the field.

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:26.880
They don't need to
have programmers.

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:30.630
They can have engineers who
know what looks bad on the graph

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:33.630
highlight that, and then expand
that out to tens of thousands

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:36.210
of metrics and be able
to quickly identify

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:37.756
things that are going wrong.

00:43:37.756 --> 00:43:40.380
And that's really exciting to be
able to scale these things out

00:43:40.380 --> 00:43:42.460
to industry.

00:43:42.460 --> 00:43:44.460
We have replication now
in Cloud Bigtable, which

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.380
is going to provide
higher availability

00:43:46.380 --> 00:43:49.620
and some great features
around workload isolation.

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:54.660
And it provides a great basis
for large scale data analysis

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:56.510
and machine learning.

00:43:56.510 --> 00:44:00.240
If you want to learn more about
the technology you saw up here,

